      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Let's dive into the nitty-gritty of the Fourier Transform. The orthogonality property is indeed the key to understanding how the integral \"cancels out\" the unwanted frequencies.\n\nIn the frequency domain, sine waves are orthogonal to each other, meaning that their product is zero. Mathematically, this is represented by the following equation:\n\n∫x(t) \\* sin(ωt) dt = 0\n\nwhere x(t) is the original signal, ω is the angular frequency, and t is time.\n\nWhen you apply the Fourier Transform to a signal x(t) that consists of multiple sine waves with different frequencies, the integral will \"pick out\" each individual frequency component. The reason for this is that the sine waves are orthogonal to each other, so when you multiply the signal with a sine wave of a different frequency, the result is zero.\n\nTo see this more clearly, consider the following example:\n\nSuppose you have a signal x(t) that consists of two sine waves:\n\nx(t) = A \\* sin(ω1t) + B \\* sin(ω2t)\n\nwhere A and B are constants, ω1 and ω2 are the angular frequencies, and t is time.\n\nWhen",
